<BaseLayout title="ST25 (Tianshu) — Heavenly Pivot Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat ST25 (Tianshu) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "ST25 (Tianshu)", href: "/points/st25-tianshu-acupressure-point" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">ST25 (Tianshu) — Heavenly Pivot</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">ST25, known as Tianshu or the Heavenly Pivot, is a central point on the Stomach meridian located beside the navel. It is the front-mu point of the Large Intestine and one of the first points acupressure guides turn to for constipation, diarrhea, bloating, and digestive discomfort.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Stomach (ST)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Abdomen, about three of your own finger-widths beside the navel, on both sides</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Constipation, diarrhea, bloating and gas, indigestion, menstrual cramps</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy — both points are reachable lying down</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">2–3 minutes per session, in slow clockwise circles</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">2–3 times daily when constipation is the concern</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">ST36 (Zusanli)</a>, <a href="/points/cv12-zhongwan-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV12 (Zhongwan)</a>, <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4 (Hegu)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Pregnancy, recent abdominal surgery or hernia near the navel, or acute severe abdominal pain</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why ST25 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">ST25 sits on the Stomach meridian beside the navel and serves as the front-mu point of the Large Intestine — the alarm point where the large intestine's qi is said to surface on the abdomen. Its name, Heavenly Pivot, describes its role as a hinge for the middle of the body, coordinating the smooth downward movement of the intestines. In traditional Chinese medicine it is the classic point for regulating bowel movement and is used for both constipation and diarrhea.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Because it sits close to the surface of the abdomen and is easy to reach, ST25 is one of the most practical points for self-care. The direction of the massage is traditionally considered important: circular movement is often described as encouraging the bowels in the direction they travel, which is why clockwise massage is frequently recommended when constipation is the concern. ST25 also appears regularly in acupressure routines for period pain, because the same region of the abdomen responds well to gentle stimulation.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/st25-tianshu-location.webp" alt="ST25 acupressure point location on the abdomen beside the navel" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="640" height="459" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">ST25 is on the abdomen, two cun lateral to the center of the belly button — about three of your own finger-widths out from the navel, on the same horizontal line. Find your navel and place three fingers beside it; the point is where the outer edge of your little finger lands, on a line level with the navel, on both the left and right sides. Pressing gently you will feel a soft tissue area just outside the belly button, and it may feel slightly more tender or "busy" than the surrounding abdomen.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) the point sits level with the navel, not above or below it; (2) the two points lie symmetrically on the left and right; (3) it feels slightly more tender or "busy" than the surrounding abdomen when pressed gently.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use ST25 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 dark:border-emerald-900/40 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You are constipated, or your bowels feel sluggish</li>
          <li>• You have loose stools and want to help settle the abdomen</li>
          <li>• You feel bloated, distended, or full of trapped gas</li>
          <li>• You want a daily digestive-maintenance point or period-cramp support</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You are pregnant — abdominal pressure is used very gently or avoided then</li>
          <li>• You have had recent abdominal surgery or have a hernia near the navel</li>
          <li>• You have severe, acute abdominal pain that is not explained</li>
          <li>• Massage makes the pain or cramping worse</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: ST25 is a gentle digestive-regulating practice, not first aid for a sudden or severe abdomen.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Constipation:</strong> ST25 is the primary acupressure point for sluggish bowels and difficulty passing stool.</li>
      <li><strong>Diarrhea:</strong> Also used traditionally to calm loose stools and settle the abdomen.</li>
      <li><strong>Bloating and gas:</strong> Helps relieve abdominal distension and trapped gas.</li>
      <li><strong>Indigestion:</strong> Traditionally used for sluggish digestion and abdominal discomfort after meals.</li>
      <li><strong>Menstrual cramps:</strong> Frequently included in routines for period pain and lower abdominal cramping.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage ST25</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">The best results come from slow, deliberate circular pressure matched to your breath. Let the abdomen stay soft and avoid forcing the point if it feels tender.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Lie on your back with your knees bent and feet flat so the abdominal wall relaxes.</li>
      <li>Place the middle fingers of both hands on the ST25 points, about three finger-widths out from the navel on each side.</li>
      <li>Apply slow, deep pressure and circle clockwise (following the natural direction of the colon) for 2–3 minutes.</li>
      <li>Keep your breathing even and relaxed, pressing a little deeper on each exhale.</li>
      <li>For constipation, repeat the clockwise massage 2–3 times daily, ideally in the morning or after a meal.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing too hard over a tender or full abdomen. ST25 works with slow, breath-matched circles, not force — and if pressure worsens the pain, stop and seek medical advice.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine ST25 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A digestive routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 2–3 minutes on the abdomen and 30–60 seconds on the limbs.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Constipation</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">ST25 (clockwise 2–3 min) → <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">ST36</a> → <a href="/points/cv12-zhongwan-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV12</a>, morning on waking and again after a meal</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Diarrhea / loose stools</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">ST25 (gentle) → <a href="/points/sp6-sanyinjiao-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">SP6</a> → ST36, using lighter pressure than for constipation</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Bloating / trapped gas</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">ST25 → CV12 → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4</a>, holding each 30–60s until the abdomen settles</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Menstrual cramps</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">ST25 → SP6 → LI4, 1–2 min each, 1–2 times daily around the period</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a complete step-by-step plan, see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-constipation" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for constipation</a> page and the <a href="/body/abdomen-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">abdomen acupressure points</a> body guide. For weight support, ST25 is also used — see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-weight-loss" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">weight loss</a> guide.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Most people notice the abdomen feels looser and less bloated within a few minutes of clockwise massage. Do not expect an immediate bowel movement — ST25 is a regulator, and changes to bowel habits take a few days of consistent use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 2–3 sessions per day, bowel regularity often improves and bloating eases. This matches the pattern in studies of abdominal acupressure for constipation, where benefits appear after several days of regular use alongside adequate hydration and fiber.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For chronic constipation, pair ST25 with the rest of your routine — fluid, fiber, and movement. Acupressure is a complement, not a replacement for medical care. If constipation persists, or you notice blood in the stool or unexplained weight loss, see a doctor rather than continuing self-treatment.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Evidence on acupressure for digestive conditions has grown in recent years. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for constipation and postoperative bowel function, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Abdominal points such as ST25 and the area around the navel appear frequently in these protocols, and the direction of the massage is often part of the intervention design.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers consistently note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated. This makes it difficult to separate the specific effects of a particular point from the general benefits of touch, pressure, and relaxation. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of encouraging signals alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["lee-frazier-2011"]} note="The review above evaluated acupressure for symptom management, including digestive complaints. It is not a study of ST25 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid strong abdominal pressure during pregnancy — ST25 should be used very gently or avoided then; consult a care provider first.</li>
      <li>Do not press if you have had recent abdominal surgery or have a hernia near the navel.</li>
      <li>Never press on acute, severe, or unexplained abdominal pain.</li>
      <li>Skip the point if there is skin irritation, a rash, or an open wound around the belly button.</li>
      <li>Stop if massage worsens abdominal pain or causes unusual cramping, and see a doctor for severe or persistent digestive symptoms.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Severe, sudden, or worsening abdominal pain</li>
        <li>Blood in the stool, or black, tarry stools</li>
        <li>Constipation or diarrhea lasting more than a few weeks, with unexplained weight loss</li>
        <li>Vomiting, fever, or an abdomen that feels hard and tender to the touch</li>
        <li>No bowel movement combined with worsening pain, bloating, or vomiting</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find ST25 on myself?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">ST25 sits about three of your own finger-widths out from the center of the navel, level with it, on both sides. Measure with your own fingers and press gently — the point may feel slightly more tender or "busy" than the surrounding abdomen.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Which direction should I massage ST25?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Traditionally, clockwise circles are used when constipation is the concern, because the movement is said to follow the natural direction of the colon. Keep the pressure slow, deep, and matched to your breath, 2–3 minutes per session.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press ST25?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For constipation, massage both points clockwise for 2–3 minutes, 2–3 times daily — ideally on waking in the morning and again after a meal. For general comfort, one slow session per day is enough. Stop if the pain worsens.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is ST25 safe during pregnancy?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Abdominal points such as ST25 are traditionally used only very gently or avoided during pregnancy. If you are pregnant, speak with your midwife or doctor before practicing acupressure on the belly.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead of using ST25?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for severe or sudden abdominal pain, blood in the stool, constipation or diarrhea with unexplained weight loss, or vomiting with a hard, tender abdomen. ST25 is a gentle supportive practice, not a substitute for medical care when these warning signs are present.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-emerald-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-emerald-400">Back to All Points</a>
